" is locked.\n        3.  Integration into existing stage-gate review processes.\n        4.  Alignment with Article 12(2) (proportionality).\n\n    *   *Institutional Survival:* Does this create a legal risk? If the system is deemed \"high-risk,\" it needs logs over its *lifetime*. However, most R&D/experimental phases aren't yet \"placed on the market\" or \"put into service.\" The EU AI Act generally applies to systems placed on the market or put into service in the EU.\n    *   *Regulatory Compliance:* Article 12 specifically targets \"High-risk AI systems.\" A system doesn't become high-risk until its *intended purpose* falls under the high-risk categories (Annex III) and it is deployed/marketed. Phasing in logging as the intended purpose crystallizes is a logical operational approach.\n    *   *Market Reputation:* Showing a systematic, gated process for compliance (rather than an afterthought) looks professional to regulators.\n\n    *   *Risk check:* Is \"lifetime\" interpreted to include the very first research epoch? Usually, the \"lifetime\" of a *high-risk AI system* starts when it's developed as such or placed on the market. However, if a prototype is used in a way that constitutes \"putting into service,\" we have a problem.\n    *   *Benefit:* Prevents \"logging bloat\" during R&D (scientific velocity) while ensuring strict adherence before deployment.\n    *   *Conclusion:* This is a sound risk-management strategy. It uses the internal lifecycle to trigger compliance requirements precisely when they become legally binding, reducing overhead without sacrificing the final regulatory target.\n\n    *   Rationale: The proposal aligns with the proportionality principle of Article 12(2) and ensures that stringent logging requirements are systematically integrated before deployment without hindering early-stage research. By linking logging depth to the \"intended purpose\" milestone, it reduces operational friction while mitigating the risk of deploying non-