s proposal isn't just about *doing* logging; it's about *researching a new way* to do logging and publishing it.\n\n    *   Article 12(3)(c) explicitly requires recording \"the input data for which the search has led to a match\" for certain biometric systems.\n    *   If the proposal uses ZKPs to avoid \"reading raw biometric inputs,\" does that contradict the requirement to *record* them? The law says \"recording of...\". Usually, regulators want to see the actual logs in a readable format during an audit. A system where you can only prove \"I did log it\" without showing *what* was logged (if the regulator demands the data) is legally risky.\n    *   Furthermore, turning a compliance requirement into a \"research contribution\" for a \"top security venue\" shifts the focus from *guaranteed compliance* to *academic prestige*. As a Compliance Officer, my priority isn't citations; it's avoiding fines and shutdowns.\n\n    *   The proposal is too risky from a pure compliance standpoint because it prioritizes privacy-preserving techniques (which may obscure necessary data) and academic publication over straightforward adherence to the recording mandates. The risk of an auditor rejecting a ZKP-based log as \"insufficient record-keeping\" outweighs the reputational gain of a paper.\n\n    *   Rationale: While technically ambitious, prioritizing research prestige and privacy-preserving obfuscation risks failing the explicit data recording requirements of Article 12(3), potentially leading to severe regulatory penalties.\n    *   Decision: Reject.\n    *   Promise: Low (maybe 20-30) because while \"privacy\" is good, \"compliance first\" is the mandate. Actually, for a Compliance Officer, an experimental approach to a hard legal requirement is often seen as a liability.\n\n    *Wait*, let me re-read Article 12(3)(c). It says \"the input data...\". If the lab implements a system where auditors *cannot* read raw inputs, they are not recording them in a way that allows for the intended traceability/verification unless the regulator accepts ZKPs as a substitute. In the current EU regulatory climate, \"black box\" or \"encrypted-only\" logs without clear access paths for authorities are usually rejected.\n\n    *Decision:* Reject.",
