ring). Generic infrastructure logs might capture \"when\" and \"where,\" but might not capture the \"what\" or \"why\" required for high-level risk assessment if the telemetry is too generic. However, providing a *standardized structured side-channel* is an excellent foundation upon which specific application-level logs can be built. Article 12(3) has very specific requirements (start/end time, reference database, etc.)—generic compute fabric logging might cover start/end times, but probably not the \"reference database\" or \"natural persons involved.\" However, as a baseline for *automatic recording* (Art 12(1)), it's a strong start.\n\n    *   Rationale: This approach implements \"compliance by design\" at the infrastructure level, ensuring that all high-risk systems inherit mandatory logging capabilities automatically and reducing the risk of individual project omissions. While application-specific logs may still be needed for Annex III requirements, this provides the necessary technical foundation required by Article 12(1) with minimal resource overhead.\n    *   Decision: Approve.\n    *   Promise: High (e.g., 90). It solves a systemic risk of non-compliance across multiple projects efficiently.\n\n    *   Rationale first? Yes.\n    *   Decision \"approve\" or \"reject\"? Yes.\n    *   Promise integer 0-100? Yes.\n    *   Echo ID exactly? Yes (P1665).",
