ons seriously from the inception of the R&D process.\n\n    *   Is it too restrictive for R&D? The proposal claims to *protect* iteration speed by removing logging design from the critical path, although enforcing schema elements on \"every new prototype\" might be perceived as friction. However, from a *Compliance Officer's* view, this friction is a feature, not a bug—it ensures compliance before the system becomes too complex to retrofit.\n    *   Does it cover all aspects? It covers timestamps (12.3a), reference databases/input matches (12.3b/c), and verification identities (12.3d).\n\n    *   Rationale: This implements \"compliance by design\" by automating the record-keeping requirements of Article 12 across the entire development pipeline, eliminating the risk of human oversight during the transition from prototype to high-risk deployment. It directly addresses the specific logging mandates for Annex III systems while minimizing manual overhead for researchers.\n    *   Decision: approve.\n    *   Promise: High (95+). It's a systemic solution rather than a per-project checklist.",
