weaving. Could be 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nCI checks for compliance data fields is fairly common practice. Not rare? Might be interesting but common. Probably 2 = interesting. Could be 3 unusual? I think CI gates for regulatory logging is somewhat common in regulated software. Maybe 2.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nStated problem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly.\nProposal does X = CI check verifies fields. Y = ensures logs present before staging. That directly addresses meeting logging mandate. So 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\nWill it solve? It verifies four minimum data fields under Article12(3). But Article12 also requires logging for all high-risk systems, not just point1(a) AnnexIII. And requires traceability etc. Also requires automatic recording over lifetime. CI check ensures fields present in prototype but does not guarantee runtime logging in production, nor Article12(2) events. So partial solution? It will contribute to solution but only partial. Might be 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Could argue it solves stated problem of meeting logging mandate for those systems? The four fields are minimum for certain systems. It ensures presence. Maybe still partial.\n\nGiven effectiveness rating: degree to which idea will solve problem. It catches gaps early but does not ensure logging over lifetime, nor automatic recording. So partial. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\nCI gate is acceptable. 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\nScript pytest suite... seems some changes but reasonably feasible. Might be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be easy? Need test fixture, throwaway instance. Probably some effort. I'd say 3.\n\nAlternatively implementability could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost. Hmm. CI check is common. Maybe 3.\n\nLet's produce output with evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Just return rating echoing id. Might list lines.\n\nProvide:\n\nid P1772\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\netc.\n\nMake one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nParadigm_relatedness_evidence: The gate embeds Article 12 logging requirements directly into the software release workflow, changing how R&D and compliance interact rather than treating logging as a post-hoc documentation task. Score 3.\n\nOriginality_evidence: Automated CI validation of regulatory log fields is a familiar DevOps practice applied to AI compliance, making it interesting but not rare or surprising. Score 2.\n\nApplicability_evidence: The proposal directly addresses the mandate to meet Article 12 logging requirements by verifying required log fields before staging. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness_evidence: It will catch missing minimum log fields early for Annex III systems but does not ensure lifetime automatic recording or broader traceability under Article 12(1)-(2). Score 3.\n\nAcceptability_evidence: Using automated tests to enforce regulatory data capture is a common, norm-conforming engineering practice. Score 4.\n\nImplementability_evidence: Building a pytest suite with synthetic queries and test fixtures requires some engineering effort and integration into CI but is reasonably feasible. Score 3.\n\nCheck ordering.\n\nReturn rating.\n\n",
