ging practice. So level 2.\n\neffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. Problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing resources. This test harness ensures logs fields present before deploy, so helps ensure compliance. Will it solve stated problem? It addresses logging mandate for Annex III biometric systems specifically. Reasonable and will contribute to solution but partial? It checks conformance but does not guarantee logs throughout lifetime, nor post-market monitoring etc. Might be partial solution. Level 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution (helps but only partial). Could be 4 solves without regard workability. If you could do it, would solve main problem? It catches non-conformance early for those four fields. But logging mandate broader than Annex III logs. So partial. Score 3.\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related? Do X to get Y and Y is part of stated problem. Yes, produce required log fields. So 4 solves identified problem directly related. Could be 4.\n\nimplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented. CI-integrated test suite schema assertions plus behavioural checks... Easy to implement at low cost or non-radical changes? Might need some changes. Probably 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Not easy low cost? It's engineering work. Could be 3. Maybe 4 easy? I'd say 3.\n\nacceptability: degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies violate no norms. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Test harness is acceptable. Score 4.\n\nProvide output with id and subscale evidence then score.\n\nNeed format: for each subscale, give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Return rating echoing its id. Provide lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1284\noriginality_evidence: ...\norigi