m\". Could be 4 = solves identified problem directly related. Hmm.\n\nThe idea applies to mandate by making compliance visible. Probably applicability 3 or 4. I'd say 3: solves implied problem of procurement scrutiny.\n\nWorkability implementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented. Build read-only dashboard showing log coverage status, yearly white paper. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Score 3? Could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Building client portal is not trivial but feasible. Might be some changes. I'd say 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Offering transparency dashboard is common, no violation. Score 4.\n\nNovelty paradigm_relatedness: degree idea preserves/modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nProposal converts regulatory obligation into competitive differentiator. That changes relationship? It creates new offering born from compliance. That sounds like paradigm stretching: changes that relationship, new partners/offerings born from compliance. Maybe 3.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare but ingenious... Unusual, interesting; shows some imagination =3? Common, mundane boring =1. Client-facing audit portal for AI logging is maybe interesting but not highly original. Could be 2 = interesting. Many companies do transparency reports. Might be common. I'd say 2 or 3. Let's choose 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence for each rating.\n\nReturn rating echoing id.\n\nFormat: probably JSON? Instruction says give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably output lines.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P2411\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 3\netc.\n\nEnsure order: effectiveness, applicability, implementability, acceptability, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The portal displays existing log coverage rather than creating the automatic event logs and specific records mandated by Article 12(1)-(3), so it supports but does not itself satisfy the logging mandate. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: It addresses the implied procurement and market trust concerns that follow from compliance, which is related to but distinct from the core requirement to technically enable logging. Score 3.\n\nImplementability evidence: Building a read-only dashboard and annual white paper requires integration with existing log stores and client communications but uses standard web and reporting tools. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Publishing anonymized schemas and retention certifications aligns with EU transparency norms and is expected by procurement officers, violating no legal or business sensibilities. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It transforms compliance into a client-facing product offering, changing the lab’s relationship to its obligations rather than merely adding logging tools. Score 3.\n\nOriginality evidence: Turning Article 12 conformance into a competitive differentiator via a public audit portal is unusual in AI R&D labs and shows imagination beyond routine internal logging. Score 3.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
