ty (1-4): degree to which idea is rare but ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane\n\nAdaptive log compression for regulatory retention is fairly common IT practice. Not rare. Might be common, mundane. Could be interesting but common. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEvidence: Log compression and tiered storage for cost control are standard data-management practices widely used by labs facing long-term retention.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult...\nProposal assigns one postdoc six months protected time. That's feasible but requires dedicated staff. Maybe 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible.\n\nEvidence: Benchmarking compression and producing a reference architecture with existing staff over six months is reasonably feasible with modest resource commitment.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, or politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nProbably 4. It's compliant in good faith.\n\nEvidence: Optimizing storage costs while retaining logs in good faith complies with Article 12 and violates no norms or sensibilities.\n\nReturn rating.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Order listed: applicability, effectiveness, paradigm_relatedness, originality, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's produce JSON? Probably just lines.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P2009\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nMake sure integer.\n\n",
