y related. So 4.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\nInternal reproducibility standard is acceptable, no violation. Common? Maybe somewhat uncommon but does not offend. Could be 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Reproducibility is norm. Might be 4.\n\nBut maybe it's internal standard raising bar above minimum compliance - acceptable. I'd say 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult...\nLightweight replay harness built on logging infrastructure... Requires building harness, changing publication workflow. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Probably 3.\n\nCould be 3.\n\nNow produce output: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nNeed order listed: paradigm_relatedness, originality, effectiveness, applicability, acceptability, implementability\n\nProvide JSON? Probably list. Format unclear. Likely return rating with fields.\n\nWe'll output something like:\n\nid: P2501\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: \"...\"\nparadigm_relatedness: 3\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The protocol embeds logging requirements into the scientific validation workflow before publication, changing how R&D and compliance obligations interact. Score 3.\n\noriginality_evidence: Requiring independent replay of logged operational data as a gate for publication is unusual for compliance responses and shows imagination beyond minimal logging. Score 3.\n\neffectiveness_evidence: If implemented, the protocol ensures logs meet Article 12 traceability and Article 72 monitoring while