ws some imagination. Not sure if ingenious enough for 4. I'd say 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, or politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\nProposal open-sources cryptographic logging, zero-knowledge proofs for auditors. Acceptable? Probably common strategies... It's privacy-preserving, aligns with GDPR. Acceptable. Uncommon but does not offend. Might be 3? Common strategies that violate no norms -> implementing logging is common. Privacy-preserving is uncommon but acceptable. Definition: 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. This is not common. So maybe 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities. Yes.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nDesign and open-source cryptographic architecture with zero-knowledge proof-based log entries, benchmark, publish in top security venue. That's significant change, expensive/difficult. Probably 2? \"significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\". Could be 2. Might be 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures. ZK proofs for logging is non-trivial research. Likely significant change and expensive. I'd say 2.\n\nCheck anchors: implementability 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible to implement. Yes.\n\nThus outputs:\n\napplicability_evidence: ... score 4\neffectiveness_evidence: ... score 4\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ... score 4\noriginality_evidence: ... score 3\nacceptability_evidence: ... score 3\nimplementability_evidence: ... score 2\n\nNeed format. Probably JSON? 