eage but Article 12 focuses on operational logs, not training. But Recital 71 mentions technical documentation includes algorithms, data, training, testing... So maybe covers.\n\nEffectiveness likely 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Evidence: It provides immutable event logs and lineage artifacts matching Article 12(3) minimums for biometric systems, directly enabling automatic recording required by Article 12.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related. 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Evidence: Building middleware that logs inference events and run lineage directly addresses the lab's need to meet Article 12 logging mandate for high-risk prototypes.\n\nNovelty originality: degree to which idea is not only rare but ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Is open-source provenance tool common? Data provenance tools exist (MLflow, Weights & Biases). Open-sourcing middleware for regulatory compliance is perhaps unusual but not extremely rare? Might be interesting. Probably originality 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Evidence: While experiment tracking exists, packaging a regulatory-grade immutable provenance layer as open-source MIT middleware specifically aligned to Article 12(3) is unusual and imaginative.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: 4 paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. 3 paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance). Proposal releases tool to community, amplifies scientific impact beyond internal publications, removes engineering bottleneck. It changes relationship? Might be paradigm stretching: logging woven into science and new offering born from compliance. Not breaking larger problem of what lab researches? It addresses reproducibility. Might be 3. Could be 2 slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. The open-source release suggests changes relationship. I'd say 3.\n\nImplementability: degree can be easily implemented. Easy to implement at low cost or non-radical changes =4. Some changes reasonably feasible =3. Significant change expensive difficult =2. Build lightweight middleware integrated into existing experiment trackers, maintain as standing lab responsibility. Probably requires some engineering effort but feasible. Maybe 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Evidence: Integrating a middleware library into existing trackers and maintaining it as standing responsibility requires dedicated engineering time but uses familiar open-source practices.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies violate no norms =4. Somewhat uncommon but does not offend =3. Offends somewhat =2. Radically violates =1. Open-sourcing compliance tool is acceptable, even positive. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Evidence: Releasing an MIT-licensed provenance library aligns with open-research norms and imposes no legal or political objection.\n\nReturn rating echo id P2594.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale, give <subscale>_evidence (one sentence) first then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably output bullet list.\n\nLet's produce:\n\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure order listed: effectiveness, applicability, originality, paradigm_relatedness, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences concise.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The tool captures immutable inference-time event logs and training lineage compatible with Article 12(3) minimums, thereby providing the automatic recording required for high-risk systems. Score 4.\n\nApplicability evidence: It directly implements logging capabilities mandated by Article 12 for biometric high-risk prototypes the lab develops. Score 4.\n\nOriginality evidence: Packaging regulatory-grade immutable provenance as an MIT-licensed middleware built on existing trackers is unusual and imaginative compared with typical internal logging scripts. Score 3.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: By weaving compliance logging into core R&D infrastructure and releasing it as a community offering, the proposal changes how the lab relates to compliance obligations. Score 3.\n\nImplementability evidence: Integration as a lightweight library into current experiment trackers is reasonably feasible with dedicated engineering effort. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Open-sourcing a compliance tool under MIT license conforms to scientific norms and raises no legal or political concerns. Score 4.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
