em of what lab researches. This seems to change relationship. Might be 4? The description for 4 says introduces new elements AND changes the relationship... or addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This does change relationship. Could be paradigm breaking. I think 3 is safer: paradigm stretching.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nEasy to implement at low cost? No, it's research investigation requiring formalising info-theoretic requirements as loss within differentiable architecture search, evaluate architectures... That's significant change, expensive/difficult but not totally impossible. So 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible. Or 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Research project is maybe significant. I'd say 2.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms =4. Investigating self-logging architectures is acceptable. So 4.\n\nNow format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nNeed echo id exactly.\n\nOutput format? Probably lines.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2580\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n\netc.\n\nEnsure order: applicability, effectiveness, originality, paradigm_relatedness, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide one sentence evidence each.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal directly targets Article 12 logging mandates by designing models that intrinsically produce the required logs. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: If realized, intrinsic logging would satisfy traceability and post-market monitoring requirements, but as a research investigation it only contributes to a future solution rather than immediate compliance. Score 3.\n\nOriginality evidence: Embedding log completeness as an information-theoretic regularizer in differentiable architecture search is unusual and im