abs do logging. Might be common. I'd say 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\nParadigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged. This is exactly standard compliance practice, logging woven in? It introduces new elements tools but still treats compliance as logging practice = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice = 2. Or paradigm preserving =1. The description says technical architecture standard requiring... integrated into CI/CD so no system passes deployment without clearing gate. That is introducing new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2 = slightly paradigm stretching.\n\nCould argue paradigm preserving because it's just compliance. Might be 1. Let's think: Dean et al. paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged. Here lab's way changes to add logging gate. So maybe slightly stretching. I'll go 2.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves identified problem directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem). Yes direct: meet logging mandate. Score 4.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\nReasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability =4. It would solve main problem? Logging coverage test gate ensures automatic recording. Might be sufficient. Probably 4.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence one sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? The prompt says return rating... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Could output as lines.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P2430\nimplementability_evidence: ...\nimplementability: 3\n...\n\nLet's ensure one sentence evidence each.\n\nImplementability evidence: Building a shared append-only microservice and CI/CD logging-coverage gate requires engineering effort but uses standard components and is reasonably feasible for an AI R&D lab.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Mandatory tamper-evident logging aligned with Article 12 is legally required and socially expected, violating no norms or sensibilities.\n\nOriginality evidence: A centralized event-logging layer with deployment gates is a common, mundane compliance engineering practice widely described in regulated software.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal adds new logging tools and pipeline processes but still treats compliance as conventional logging practice rather than changing the lab’s R&D-compliance relationship.\n\nApplicability evidence: The idea directly provides automatic event logs for operational sessions and risk triggers mandated by Article 12(1)-(2), addressing the stated logging mandate.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: If implemented, the architecture would ensure every high-risk system can automatically record required events, thereby solving the core compliance gap.\n\nScores as above.\n\nCheck anchors: implementability 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Yes.\n\nacceptability 4 = common strategies that violate no norms.\n\noriginality 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nparadigm_relatedness 2 = slightly paradigm stretching.\n\napplicability 4 = solves identified problem directly related.\n\neffectiveness 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
