ategies that do not offend sensibilities=3; offends somewhat=2; radically violates=1\n\nLogging middleware is acceptable, common. So 4.\n\napplicability: solves identified problem directly related to stated problem =4; solves implied problem related=3; may have some benefit within special situation=2; intervention not stated=1\n\nProposal directly addresses logging mandate and meeting Article 12 requirements. That's directly related. So 4.\n\neffectiveness: reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability =4; reasonable and will contribute to solution =3; unreasonable unlikely =2; solves unrelated =1\n\nWill it solve? It auto-captures required event types and Annex III minimums, version-control schema. Seems will solve main problem of meeting logging mandate while keeping velocity. Reasonable and will solve stated problem. So 4.\n\nNow produce output with evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? The instruction says return rating... Probably list.\n\nI'll output:\n\nid: P2652\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft sentences.\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal adds a shared logging tool but still treats compliance as a logging practice rather than changing what the lab researches. => score 2\n\noriginality_evidence: A reusable instrumentation library for compliance logging is a common engineering pattern in R&D labs. => score 1? If we chose 1. Hmm.\n\nLet's decide originality = 2 (interesting). Could be 2.\n\nMaybe better to be conservative: originality 2.\n\nImplementability_evidence: Integrating middleware into an existing prototyping SDK requires engineering work but is reasonably feasible without radical architecture changes. => score 3\n\nAcceptability_evidence: Automatic structured logging of system events is le