n into science, new partners...). 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. 1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged.\n\nProposal embeds logging as default module in every build, refactor codebase templates. That's introducing new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. It's slightly paradigm stretching? It is weaving logging into engineering process from day one. Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious... 4 = not expressed before AND ingenious... 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. 2 = interesting. 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nEmbedding telemetry layer as default module is fairly common practice for compliance. Might be common, mundane. Could be 1 or 2. Probably common, mundane, boring. Implement logging by default is standard. So originality 1.\n\nImplementability: degree to which idea can be easily implemented. 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult... Proposal: two senior engineers lead six-week sprint, reusing current experiment-tracker infrastructure rather than procuring new tooling. Refactor codebase templates so logging middleware instantiated at initialization. That's some changes, reasonably feasible. Could be 3. Not easy low cost? Six-week sprint with two seniors is moderate. Might be 3.\n\nAcceptability: degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. This is compliant with law. Acceptable. So 4.\n\nWe must give evidence sentence first then score.\n\nFormat? Probably output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Likely as lines.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P2182\neffectiveness_evidence: ... \neffectiveness: 3\n...\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The unified telemetry layer provides automatic start/end timestamps and reference-database queries, directly addressing Article 12 logging requirements and reducing retrofit cost, but it does not explicitly ensure identification of natural persons verifying results or full post-market monitoring traces. Score 3.\n\nApplicability evidence: Embedding logging as a default module in every high-risk system build directly targets the mandate to technically allow automatic event recording over the system's lifetime. Score 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: The proposal introduces a reusable logging middleware and template refactor but still treats compliance as a standard logging practice rather than changing the lab’s research-compliance relationship. Score 2.\n\nOriginality evidence: Building automatic telemetry into system templates by reusing existing experiment-tracker infrastructure is a common, mundane engineering approach for regulatory logging. Score 1.\n\nImplementability evidence: A six-week sprint led by two senior engineers reusing current tooling represents reasonably feasible measures with moderate change and cost. Score 3.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Integrating automatic logs to meet EU AI Act record-keeping requirements is a legally compliant, socially neutral engineering practice. Score 4.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
